  "worked_solution": "We wish to express $y(x)$ as a Taylor series about $x=0$:\n\n$$\ny(x)=y(0)+x{y}^{\u2032}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{\u2033}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+\u22ef\n$$\n\nWe're given $y(0)$ and ${y}^{\u2032}(0),$ so we need to calculate ${y}^{\u2033}(0)$ and ${y}^{(3)}(0).$\n\nWe know that $y(0)=3,$ so we substitute this into the given equation to find ${y}^{\u2033}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2033}(0) & =5y(0)-2(0)^{2} \\\\  & =5(3)+0 \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to find an expression for ${y}^{(3)}(x),$ we differentiate both sides on the given equation with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n{y}^{(3)}=5{y}^{\u2032}-4x\n$$\n\nWe can now find  ${y}^{(3)}(0)$ by substituting in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{(3)}(0) & =5{y}^{\u2032}(0)-4(0) \\\\  & =5(0)-0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we  substitute  the obtained values in  the Taylor series:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =y(0)+x{y}^{\u2032}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}{y}^{\u2033}(0)+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}{y}^{(3)}(0)+\u22ef \\\\  & =3+x\\cdot 0+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}\\cdot 15+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}\\cdot 0+\u22ef \\\\  & =3+\\frac{15{x}^{2}}{2}+\u22ef\\end{aligned}\n$$",
